General csv Files and what to expect inside each one:

csv_editing.py:
This file exists for insights into the csv files that exist. This ensures that there is enough data
To be reasonably used for training adn what the general trends look like
This file is good to identify issues with a training set before it reaches the AI

    This file creates the following files currently in the repository

    - Crashes_by_city_and_day.csv/xisx: csv is a csv format while xisx is meant to be viewed
    in excel. Compares the amount of crashes in each city each day

    - Crashes_by_road.csv/xisx: Provides a long list of each road and how many crashes were reported on
    each one. The function that created should return the median and average crashes per road.
    This is good for assigning non-urgent roads inside of csv_overview.py

csv_overview.py:
This file exists to manipulate the data inside of a .csv file to create training data for the ai
This file takes several minutes to fully run depending on the intial size of the csv file given to it

    Patience is important when running this file

    This file creates several CSVs. The naming convention is under the impression that the first csv will be named in the schema of:
    <CountyName>County<Year>.csv

    As an example DallasCounty2025.csv will be used

    The follow files are created in order:
    - DallasCounty2025_Modified.csv
      <CountyName>County<Year>_Modified.csv

      This file replaces the roads with less than the specified number of crashes with 'Non Critial Roads'
      The point of this is to reduce the amount of roads that need to exprience time slicing. This could mean millions fewer entires in the training data

    - DallasCounty2025_Modified_with_crash_labels.csv
      <CountyName>County<Year>_Modified_with_crash_labels.csv

      This file adds the crash column to the data to signify that a crash occured on this road. This will be important as negative sampling
      which is a way to train AI needs to have some examples of when crashes didn't happen and this provides that difference

    - road_reference.csv

      This creates a list of all the roads and their general features such as if they are urban or rural as well as what city/county they are in
      This will have a lot of No Data for the rural/urban flag
      Will be important to generate consistant negative data

    - negative_samples.csv

      This creates the negative samples using road_reference and the <CountyName>County<Year>_Modified_with_crash_labels.csv
      This will create for hour inside the year for each road listed in the modified data an incident of a crash not occuring
      At present, the weather and road conditions are randomized, but this will be corrected

    - TrainingData.csv

      This combines the modified data and the negative samples
      This does remove negative samples that happen in the same hour as positive (crash) samples
      This takes the most amount of time to load, but is the final product
